{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785185876,"nonce":"BwiCed+zGe0srszsKpFY1Jvcp64=","message":"**As specified, this spike cannot falsify the design** — red-team finding against ADR-0062.\n\nOperations-per-second under latency is the right *headline* number and it exercises none of the things most likely to sink the design: `EXDEV`/redirect correctness on inherited directory renames, whiteout and opaque handling in the drain, Farm eviction under a live mount, an `nfsd` restart with live Exports, or cross-AZ behaviour.\n\nThe rung guard is also narrower than it reads. It covers reflink and `overlayfs`, but not `redirect_dir`, not **which drain ran** (exact change set vs stat-cache fallback), and not whether the mount was actually **NFS** — and it now must, because a mount can silently fail all the way to a Pod wedged in `ContainerCreating` when the node lacks an NFS client (see 99a63d8).\n\nAdd to the acceptance criteria: the run must record, and assert, the whole configuration it measured — rung, drain, mount type, `redirect_dir`, and kernel. A spike shaped only to produce a number will produce one.","files":null}]}